<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as poleis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A polis was a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>, a self-governing urban community with its surrounding territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> founded and supported many Greek cities across its realm. These poleis served as centres of administration, trade and Hellenistic culture, and enjoyed varying degrees of local self-government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polis.jpg" alt="Polis" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Like other Cretan <b>poleis</b>, Knossos fielded a citizen army trained through the agelai and men's houses, and Cretans were famed above all as archers and light troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucids founded Greek and Macedonian military colonies (katoikiai) and new cities (<b>poleis</b>) across their domains as instruments of military control and economic development; Appian credits Seleucus I alone with nine cities named Seleucia and sixteen named Antioch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Danish historian Mogens Herman Hansen, who led the Copenhagen <b>Polis</b> Centre’s comparison of some thirty city-state cultures, defined it as “a self-governing, but not necessarily independent, political unit”: many city-states acknowledged an overlord or belonged to a league while running their own affairs.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 700 BCE most <b>poleis</b> were governed by narrow circles of landowning families who had displaced or absorbed their early kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew into a city in the early Iron Age, and by the 7th century BCE it was one of the important <b>poleis</b> of Crete.</blockquote><a href="/state/eleutherna">Eleutherna</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy II refounded the site as Arsinoe c. 270 BCE, and the modern town of <b>Polis</b> stands there today.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-marion">Kingdom of Marion</a></li></ul></section>
